                </span>
<h2>History and Development of Computer Systems</h2>
<h3>Early Development of Computers</h3>
<p>
                    Computers have developed through many
                    generations. Early computing devices
                    were simple tools designed to help
                    people perform calculations.
                </p>
<p>
                    Over time, new inventions made computers
                    faster, smaller, more powerful, and
                    easier to use.
                </p>
<h3>First Generation (1940–1956)</h3>
<p>
                    The first generation of computers used
                    vacuum tubes to process information.
                    These computers were very large,
                    consumed a lot of electricity,
                    and produced significant heat.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
                    ENIAC, one of the early general-purpose
                    electronic computers.
                </div>
<h3>Second Generation (1956–1963)</h3>
<p>
                    The second generation used transistors
                    instead of vacuum tubes. Transistors
                    made computers smaller, more reliable,
                    and more energy-efficient.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
                    IBM 1401.
                </div>
<h3>Third Generation (1964–1971)</h3>
<p>
                    Integrated circuits became important
                    during this generation. Several electronic
                    components could be placed on a single
                    chip, improving performance and
                    reducing computer size.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
                    IBM System/360.
                </div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                 LESSON 5
            ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="page-5">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 05</span>
<h2>Basic Components of a Computer System</h2>
<p>
                    A computer system contains different components
                    that work together to process information.
                    Each component has a specific function,
                    and the computer needs these parts to
                    perform its operations correctly.
                </p>
<h3>Major Components</h3>
<h3>1. Central Processing Unit (CPU)</h3>
<p>
                    The CPU executes instructions and processes
                    information. It is commonly called the brain
                    of the computer because it controls many
                    important operations.
                </p>
<h3>2. Memory (RAM)</h3>
<p>
                    RAM temporarily stores data and programs
                    that are currently being used by the computer.
                </p>
<h3>3. Storage Device</h3>
<p>
                    Storage devices keep files, applications,
                    and the operating system even when the
                    computer is turned off.
                </p>
<p>
                    Examples include Solid State Drives (SSD)
                    and Hard Disk Drives (HDD).
                </p>
<h3>4. Motherboard</h3>
<p>
                    The motherboard is the main circuit board
                    that connects the CPU, memory, storage,
                    and other hardware components.
                </p>
<h3>5. Power Supply Unit (PSU)</h3>
<p>
                    The power supply provides electrical power
                    to the computer's internal components.
                </p>
<h3>6. Graphics Processing Unit (GPU)</h3>
<p>
                    The GPU handles graphics-related tasks,
                    including displaying images, rendering
                    videos, and supporting certain scientific
                    calculations.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example: Playing a Computer Game</h3>
<ul>
<li>The CPU processes the game's instructions.</li>
<li>RAM holds the game data currently needed.</li>
<li>The GPU renders the graphics.</li>
<li>The storage device contains the installed game.</li>
<li>The monitor displays the result.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    Each component contributes to the overall
                    performance of a computer. If one important
                    component fails, the computer may become
                    slow or stop working properly.
                </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
