<section class="chapter" id="page-6">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 06</span>
<h2>Central Processing Unit (CPU)</h2>
<h3>What Is a CPU?</h3>
<p>
                    The Central Processing Unit, commonly known
                    as the CPU, is the main processing component
                    of a computer. It executes instructions from
                    programs and performs calculations and
                    logical operations.
                </p>
<p>
                    The CPU communicates with memory and other
                    computer components to complete different
                    tasks. Its performance affects how quickly
                    a computer can process instructions.
                </p>
<h3>Main Functions of the CPU</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Instruction Processing:</strong>
                        Executes instructions provided by
                        computer programs.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Data Calculation:</strong>
                        Performs mathematical operations.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Logical Operations:</strong>
                        Compares values and makes logical decisions.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>System Control:</strong>
                        Coordinates different activities
                        inside the computer.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Data Movement:</strong>
                        Transfers information between registers,
                        memory, and other components.
                    </li>
</ol>
<h3>Important CPU Features</h3>
<h3>CPU Cores</h3>
<p>
                    A core is an individual processing unit
                    inside a CPU. Multiple cores allow a
                    processor to work on different tasks
                    at the same time.
                </p>
<h3>Clock Speed</h3>
<p>
                    Clock speed describes the rate at which
                    a processor operates, commonly measured
                    in gigahertz (GHz).
                </p>
<p>
                    It is one performance factor but does not
                    determine overall performance by itself.
                </p>
<h3>Cache Memory</h3>
<p>
                    Cache is a small and very fast memory
                    that keeps frequently needed information
                    close to the processor.
                </p>
<h3>Threads</h3>
<p>
                    Threads are sequences of instructions
                    that can be scheduled for execution.
                    Some CPUs support simultaneous
                    multithreading to handle multiple
                    threads efficiently.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        When a user opens a calculator and
                        enters 25 + 15, the CPU processes
                        the instructions and calculates
                        the answer, which is 40.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance of the CPU</h3>
<p>
                    Without a processor, a general-purpose
                    computer cannot execute its programs.
                    It plays a central role in running
                    applications, managing instructions,
                    and processing information.
                </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                 LESSON 7
            ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="page-7">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 07</span>
<h2>Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</h2>
<h3>What Is an ALU?</h3>
<p>
                    The Arithmetic Logic Unit is a major
                    part of the CPU responsible for performing
                    mathematical and logical operations.
                </p>
<p>
                    It processes data based on instructions
                    received from the control unit.
                    The ALU works with values stored in
                    registers and sends the results back
                    to the processor's internal storage
                    or memory.
                </p>
<h3>Main Functions of the ALU</h3>
<h3>1. Arithmetic Operations</h3>
<p>
                    The ALU performs mathematical calculations
                    such as:
                </p>
<ul>
<li>Addition</li>
<li>Subtraction</li>
<li>Multiplication</li>
<li>Division</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Logical Operations</h3>
<p>
                    The ALU performs logical operations
                    used when comparing or evaluating information.
                </p>
<ul>
<li>AND</li>
<li>OR</li>
<li>NOT</li>
<li>XOR</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Comparison Operations</h3>
<p>
                    The ALU can compare two values to
                    determine whether one is greater than,
                    less than, or equal to another.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example: Addition</h3>
<p>
                        Suppose a computer needs to calculate:
                    </p>
<pre><code>25 + 15 = 40</code></pre>
<p>
                        The CPU receives the instruction,
                        and the ALU performs the addition.
                        The result is then stored temporarily
                        or sent to memory for further use.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance of the ALU</h3>
<p>
                    The ALU allows computers to perform
                    calculations and make logical comparisons.
                    These functions are necessary for calculators,
                    spreadsheets, games, business applications,
                    and programming operations.
                </p>
</section>
