<section class="chapter" id="page-8">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 08</span>
<h2>Control Unit (CU) and Registers</h2>
<h3>Control Unit</h3>
<p>
                    The Control Unit is a component of the
                    CPU that manages and coordinates
                    instruction execution.
                </p>
<p>
                    It directs the activities of other
                    processor components and controls
                    the movement of information.
                </p>
<p>
                    It does not perform most mathematical
                    calculations itself. Instead, it determines
                    what operation should happen and coordinates
                    the components responsible for carrying it out.
                </p>
<h3>Functions of the Control Unit</h3>
<ul>
<li>Retrieves instructions from memory.</li>
<li>Decodes instructions to understand their meaning.</li>
<li>Sends control signals to other components.</li>
<li>Coordinates the execution of instructions.</li>
<li>Manages the movement of data within the processor.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Registers</h3>
<p>
                    Registers are small, high-speed storage
                    locations inside the CPU.
                </p>
<p>
                    They temporarily hold information needed
                    during instruction execution.
                </p>
<h3>Common Types of Registers</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Register</th>
<th>Function</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Program Counter (PC)</td>
<td>
                                Stores the address of the next
                                instruction that the CPU should execute.
                            </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Instruction Register (IR)</td>
<td>
                                Holds the instruction currently
                                being processed.
                            </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory Address Register (MAR)</td>
<td>
                                Holds the address of the memory
                                location being accessed.
                            </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory Data Register (MDR)</td>
<td>
                                Temporarily holds data being
                                transferred to or from memory.
                            </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Accumulator (ACC)</td>
<td>
                                Stores intermediate results of
                                arithmetic and logical operations
                                in processor designs that use
                                an accumulator.
                            </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        When a computer adds two numbers,
                        registers temporarily hold the values.
                        The control unit directs the operation,
                        and the ALU performs the addition.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    The Control Unit and registers help the
                    CPU execute instructions in an organized
                    and efficient manner.
                </p>
</section>
