<section class="chapter" id="page-9">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 09</span>
<h2>Memory Systems</h2>
<h3>What Is Computer Memory?</h3>
<p>
                    Computer memory is the part of a computer
                    system that stores data and instructions
                    needed during processing.
                </p>
<p>
                    It allows the CPU to access information
                    required by programs.
                </p>
<p>
                    Memory systems are organized into
                    different levels based on their speed,
                    capacity, and purpose.
                </p>
<h3>Types of Computer Memory</h3>
<h3>1. Registers</h3>
<p>
                    Registers are located inside the CPU.
                    They provide very fast temporary storage
                    for the processor.
                </p>
<h3>2. Cache Memory</h3>
<p>
                    Cache stores frequently used data and
                    instructions so the CPU can access
                    them quickly.
                </p>
<h3>3. RAM</h3>
<p>
                    Random Access Memory temporarily holds
                    programs and data currently in use.
                </p>
<h3>4. ROM</h3>
<p>
                    Read-Only Memory is non-volatile memory
                    used to store firmware and other
                    essential instructions.
                </p>
<h3>Memory Hierarchy</h3>
<p>
                    The memory hierarchy organizes storage
                    based on speed and closeness to the CPU.
                </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Memory Type</th>
<th>General Speed</th>
<th>Main Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Registers</td>
<td>Fastest</td>
<td>Immediate CPU operations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cache</td>
<td>Very fast</td>
<td>Frequently used data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>RAM</td>
<td>Fast</td>
<td>Active programs and data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SSD/HDD</td>
<td>Slower</td>
<td>Long-term storage</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        When a student opens a presentation
                        program, its files are stored on an SSD.
                    </p>
<p>
                        When the program runs, necessary data
                        is loaded into RAM. The CPU then uses
                        cache and registers while processing
                        instructions.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    Memory allows the computer to access
                    information when needed.
                </p>
<p>
                    Proper memory management helps improve
                    system performance and application
                    responsiveness.
                </p>
</section>
