<section class="chapter" id="page-10">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 10</span>
<h2>Primary Memory: RAM and ROM</h2>
<h3>Random Access Memory (RAM)</h3>
<p>
                    RAM is the main temporary memory used
                    by a computer while programs are running.
                </p>
<p>
                    It stores the information that the CPU
                    needs to access during active tasks.
                </p>
<p>
                    RAM is volatile memory, meaning its
                    stored contents are normally lost
                    when the computer loses power.
                </p>
<h3>Functions of RAM</h3>
<ul>
<li>Holds currently running applications.</li>
<li>Stores temporary working data.</li>
<li>Supports multitasking.</li>
<li>
                        Provides the CPU with quick access
                        to active information.
                    </li>
</ul>
<h3>Types of RAM</h3>
<h3>1. DRAM (Dynamic Random Access Memory)</h3>
<p>
                    DRAM is commonly used as the main
                    memory in computers.
                </p>
<p>
                    It requires regular refreshing to
                    maintain stored information.
                </p>
<h3>2. SRAM (Static Random Access Memory)</h3>
<p>
                    SRAM is faster and commonly used
                    for CPU cache.
                </p>
<p>
                    It does not require the same refreshing
                    process as DRAM while powered.
                </p>
<h3>Read-Only Memory (ROM)</h3>
<p>
                    ROM refers to non-volatile memory that
                    retains stored information without
                    continuous power.
                </p>
<p>
                    It is commonly used for firmware
                    and startup-related instructions.
                </p>
<h3>RAM vs. ROM</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>RAM</th>
<th>ROM</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Usually temporary</td>
<td>Retains information</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Volatile</td>
<td>Non-volatile</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Used for active programs</td>
<td>Often used for firmware</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Supports reading and writing</td>
<td>
                                Writing depends on ROM technology
                            </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        When a computer starts, firmware
                        helps initialize the hardware.
                    </p>
<p>
                        After startup, the operating system
                        loads into RAM so the user can
                        work with applications.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    RAM supports active tasks, while ROM
                    and other non-volatile firmware storage
                    help provide essential startup instructions.
                </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                 LESSON 11
            ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="page-11">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 11</span>
<h2>Secondary Storage Devices</h2>
<h3>What Is Secondary Storage?</h3>
<p>
                    Secondary storage refers to devices used
                    to keep information for long periods.
                </p>
<p>
                    Unlike RAM, secondary storage can retain
                    files even when the computer is turned off.
                </p>
<p>
                    It is used to save documents, photos,
                    videos, applications, operating systems,
                    and other important information.
                </p>
<h3>Types of Secondary Storage</h3>
<h3>1. Hard Disk Drive (HDD)</h3>
<p>
                    An HDD stores information on magnetic
                    rotating disks. It is commonly used
                    for storing large amounts of data.
                </p>
<h3>2. Solid State Drive (SSD)</h3>
<p>
                    An SSD uses flash memory to store
                    information. It has no moving mechanical
                    parts and generally provides faster
                    access than traditional HDDs.
                </p>
<h3>3. USB Flash Drive</h3>
<p>
                    A USB flash drive is a small portable
                    storage device that connects through
                    a USB port.
                </p>
<h3>4. Memory Card</h3>
<p>
                    Memory cards are small storage devices
                    commonly used in cameras, smartphones,
                    and other portable electronics.
                </p>
<h3>5. Optical Discs</h3>
<p>
                    CDs, DVDs, and Blu-ray discs store
                    information using optical technology.
                </p>
<h3>HDD vs. SSD</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>HDD</th>
<th>SSD</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Uses moving mechanical parts</td>
<td>Uses flash memory</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Generally slower</td>
<td>Generally faster</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
                                Often lower cost per storage capacity
                            </td>
<td>
                                Often higher cost per capacity
                            </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
                                Can be more sensitive to
                                physical movement
                            </td>
<td>
                                Has no moving disk parts
                            </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        A student saves a research paper
                        on an SSD.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Later, the student can reopen
                        the file even after shutting
                        down the computer.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    Secondary storage protects information
                    from being lost when the computer
                    is turned off.
                </p>
<p>
                    It also provides space for applications
                    and large files.
                </p>
</section>
