<section class="chapter" id="page-16">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 16</span>
<h2>Von Neumann Architecture</h2>
<h3>What Is Von Neumann Architecture?</h3>
<p>
                    Von Neumann Architecture is a computer
                    design in which program instructions
                    and data are stored in the same
                    memory system.
                </p>
<p>
                    It is based on the stored-program
                    concept, which allows computers to
                    store instructions in memory and
                    execute them automatically.
                </p>
<p>
                    The architecture is named after
                    mathematician John von Neumann,
                    whose work helped establish the
                    stored-program computer model.
                </p>
<h3>Main Components</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Input Unit:</strong>
                        Receives data and instructions
                        from users or external devices.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Memory Unit:</strong>
                        Stores both program instructions
                        and data.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Control Unit:</strong>
                        Directs the execution of instructions.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Arithmetic Logic Unit:</strong>
                        Performs calculations and logical
                        operations.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Output Unit:</strong>
                        Presents processed information
                        to the user.
                    </li>
</ol>
<h3>How It Works</h3>
<ol>
<li>The computer receives input.</li>
<li>
                        Instructions and data are stored
                        in memory.
                    </li>
<li>
                        The CPU fetches an instruction.
                    </li>
<li>
                        The Control Unit decodes the instruction.
                    </li>
<li>
                        The ALU or another CPU component
                        performs the required operation.
                    </li>
<li>
                        The result is stored or sent to
                        an output device.
                    </li>
</ol>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        A desktop computer running a calculator
                        application can follow the stored-program
                        model. The program instructions and data
                        are held in memory and processed
                        by the CPU.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Simple and organized design.</li>
<li>
                        Shared memory can be used for
                        both instructions and data.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Flexible for general-purpose computing.
                    </li>
</ul>
<h3>Limitation</h3>
<p>
                    Instructions and data may compete for
                    access to shared memory pathways.
                    This limitation is commonly called
                    the Von Neumann bottleneck.
                </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                 LESSON 17
            ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="page-17">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 17</span>
<h2>Harvard Architecture</h2>
<h3>What Is Harvard Architecture?</h3>
<p>
                    Harvard Architecture is a computer design
                    that uses separate memory systems for
                    program instructions and data.
                </p>
<p>
                    Unlike Von Neumann Architecture, it provides
                    separate pathways for accessing instructions
                    and data. This can allow both to be accessed
                    at the same time.
                </p>
<h3>Main Components</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Program Memory:</strong>
                        Stores the instructions that the
                        processor needs to execute.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Data Memory:</strong>
                        Stores the information used
                        by the program.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Processor:</strong>
                        Executes instructions and
                        processes data.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Separate Buses:</strong>
                        Provide different communication
                        pathways for instructions and data.
                    </li>
</ol>
<h3>How It Works</h3>
<ol>
<li>
                        The processor retrieves an instruction
                        from program memory.
                    </li>
<li>
                        At the same time, it can access
                        information from data memory.
                    </li>
<li>
                        The processor executes the instruction.
                    </li>
<li>
                        The result is stored in the
                        appropriate location.
                    </li>
</ol>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        Some microcontrollers use Harvard-style
                        designs to efficiently process
                        instructions and data.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Digital signal processing systems
                        may also use separate instruction
                        and data pathways.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                        Allows simultaneous instruction
                        and data access.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Can improve performance in
                        suitable applications.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Provides separate memory organization.
                    </li>
</ul>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                        Hardware design can be more complex.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Separate memory spaces may require
                        additional design considerations.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Memory capacity may be less flexible
                        between instructions and data.
                    </li>
</ul>
<h3>Von Neumann vs. Harvard Architecture</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Von Neumann</th>
<th>Harvard</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Shared memory</td>
<td>
                                Separate instruction and
                                data memory
                            </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Common pathways</td>
<td>Separate pathways</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Simpler organization</td>
<td>More specialized organization</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>General-purpose model</td>
<td>
                                Common in specialized
                                and embedded designs
                            </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
