<section class="chapter" id="page-18">
<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 18</span>
<h2>Instruction Cycle</h2>
<h3>What Is the Instruction Cycle?</h3>
<p>
                    The instruction cycle is the process
                    followed by a CPU when executing
                    program instructions.
                </p>
<p>
                    It explains how the processor retrieves,
                    understands, and performs commands.
                </p>
<p>
                    The process repeats continuously while
                    the computer runs programs.
                </p>
<h3>Four Main Stages</h3>
<h3>1. Fetch</h3>
<p>
                    The CPU retrieves the next instruction
                    from memory. The Program Counter helps
                    identify the instruction's address.
                </p>
<h3>2. Decode</h3>
<p>
                    The Control Unit interprets the instruction
                    to determine what operation is required.
                </p>
<h3>3. Execute</h3>
<p>
                    The CPU performs the required task.
                    It may involve the ALU, memory,
                    or other processor components.
                </p>
<h3>4. Store</h3>
<p>
                    The result is saved in a register
                    or memory when necessary.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example: Addition</h3>
<pre><code>10 + 5 = 15</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Fetch:</strong>
                            The CPU retrieves the addition instruction.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Decode:</strong>
                            The Control Unit recognizes the operation.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Execute:</strong>
                            The ALU calculates the answer.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Store:</strong>
                            The result, 15, is saved in
                            a register or memory.
                        </li>
</ul>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    The instruction cycle is the foundation
                    of CPU operation. It allows the processor
                    to execute program instructions
                    in an organized manner.
                </p>
</section>
