<h3>What Is an Operating System?</h3>
<p>
                    An operating system (OS) is system software
                    that manages computer hardware and software
                    resources. It provides the environment
                    where applications can run.
                </p>
<p>
                    The operating system acts as an intermediary
                    between users, applications, and
                    computer hardware.
                </p>
<h3>Main Functions of an Operating System</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Process Management:</strong>
                        Manages running programs and distributes
                        CPU time among processes.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Memory Management:</strong>
                        Controls how memory is allocated
                        and used by applications.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>File Management:</strong>
                        Organizes files and folders and
                        manages access to stored information.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Device Management:</strong>
                        Coordinates communication between
                        the operating system and hardware devices.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>User Interface:</strong>
                        Provides a way for users to interact
                        with the computer.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Security Management:</strong>
                        Helps protect files, accounts,
                        and system resources through access
                        controls and other security features.
                    </li>
</ol>
