<h2>32-Bit and 64-Bit Systems</h2>
<h3>What Are 32-Bit and 64-Bit Systems?</h3>
<p>
                    A bit is a binary digit that can have
                    a value of either 0 or 1.
                </p>
<p>
                    In computer architecture, 32-bit and
                    64-bit describe certain processor and
                    system characteristics, including the
                    width of registers and the architecture's
                    addressing capabilities.
                </p>
<h3>32-Bit Systems</h3>
<p>
                    A 32-bit system uses a 32-bit architecture
                    for important processor operations.
                </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                        Uses 32-bit general-purpose registers
                        in its 32-bit execution mode.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Has more limited address space
                        than a typical 64-bit system.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Supports software designed for
                        compatible 32-bit environments.
                    </li>
</ul>
<h3>64-Bit Systems</h3>
<p>
                    A 64-bit system supports wider registers
                    and a much larger potential address space.
                </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                        Uses 64-bit general-purpose registers
                        in its 64-bit execution mode.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Can support much larger amounts
                        of memory, depending on the processor
                        and operating system.
                    </li>
<li>
                        Can handle many modern computing
                        requirements more effectively.
                    </li>
</ul>
<h3>Comparison</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>32-Bit</th>
<th>64-Bit</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>32-bit architecture</td>
<td>64-bit architecture</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>More limited address space</td>
<td>Much larger address space</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Supports compatible 32-bit software</td>
<td>Supports compatible 64-bit software</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
                                Limited memory capacity
                                in common configurations
                            </td>
<td>
                                Supports larger memory capacities
                            </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        A modern computer with a 64-bit processor
                        and operating system can support
                        applications designed for
                        64-bit environments.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Some systems also provide compatibility
                        for older 32-bit applications.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    Understanding these architectures helps
                    users choose compatible software,
                    operating systems, and hardware.
                </p>
</section>
