<span class="chapter-number">LESSON 25</span>
<h2>Virtualization and Cloud Computing</h2>
<h3>What Is Virtualization?</h3>
<p>
                    Virtualization is a technology that allows
                    one physical computer to operate multiple
                    virtual computing environments.
                </p>
<p>
                    A virtual machine (VM) behaves like a
                    separate computer with its own operating
                    system and allocated resources.
                </p>
<h3>Main Components of Virtualization</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Host Machine:</strong>
                        The physical computer where
                        virtualization software operates.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Hypervisor:</strong>
                        Software or firmware that creates
                        and manages virtual machines.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Virtual Machine:</strong>
                        A software-based computer environment
                        with virtual hardware resources.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Guest Operating System:</strong>
                        The operating system installed
                        inside a virtual machine.
                    </li>
</ol>
<h3>Types of Hypervisors</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Type 1:</strong>
                        Runs directly on the physical hardware.
                    </li>
<li>
<strong>Type 2:</strong>
                        Runs as an application on top of
                        an existing operating system.
                    </li>
</ul>
<h3>What Is Cloud Computing?</h3>
<p>
                    Cloud computing provides computing services
                    through a network, commonly the Internet.
                </p>
<p>
                    These services may include storage,
                    servers, databases, networking,
                    and software.
                </p>
<h3>Main Cloud Service Models</h3>
<h3>1. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)</h3>
<p>
                    Provides virtual computing resources
                    such as servers, storage, and networking.
                </p>
<h3>2. Platform as a Service (PaaS)</h3>
<p>
                    Provides an environment for developing
                    and deploying applications.
                </p>
<h3>3. Software as a Service (SaaS)</h3>
<p>
                    Provides software applications
                    through the Internet.
                </p>
<p>
                    Examples include online document editors
                    and web-based email services.
                </p>
<div class="example-box">
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        A company may use cloud computing
                        to store files and run applications
                        without purchasing and maintaining
                        every physical server itself.
                    </p>
</div>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<p>
                    Virtualization and cloud computing help
                    organizations use computing resources
                    more flexibly and efficiently.
                </p>
</section>
